# a-generic-says-what-each-call-must-give-it

Generic functions over mailboxes and threads: under each, the reference
`heroes doc` prints says what each call must give its type parameters, every
rule of spec § 11 its body asks of them and the operation that asks it,
carried in from a generic it calls too, and nothing under a generic that
asks nothing (panel 256's R4).

## function relay

    function relay<T>(m: (mailbox T), v: T) -> bool

Sends a copy of `v` into `m`.

- `m`: the mailbox
- `v`: the value, left as it was

    relay(m, v)

What each call must give its type parameters (spec § 11):

- `T` reaches no `ptr` and no `cstr`: `(mailbox T)` makes it cross between threads, line 11
- `T` is not `()`: `(mailbox T)` is a mailbox of it, line 11
- `T` holds no C handle and no thread: `m.send(v)` copies it, line 12

## function hand

    function hand<T>(m: (mailbox T), @v: T) -> bool

Hands `v` over into `m`, emptying it.

- `m`: the mailbox
- `v`: the value, emptied

    hand(m, @v)

What each call must give its type parameters (spec § 11):

- `T` reaches no `ptr` and no `cstr`: `(mailbox T)` makes it cross between threads, line 18
- `T` is not `()`: `(mailbox T)` is a mailbox of it, line 18
- `T` holds a C handle or a thread: `m.send(@v)` hands it over, line 19

## function hand_on

    function hand_on<A>(m: (mailbox A), @v: A) -> bool

Hands `v` over through `hand`.

- `m`: the mailbox
- `v`: the value, emptied

    hand_on(m, @v)

What each call must give its type parameters (spec § 11):

- `A` reaches no `ptr` and no `cstr`: `(mailbox A)` makes it cross between threads, line 25
- `A` is not `()`: `(mailbox A)` is a mailbox of it, line 25
- `A` holds a C handle or a thread: `hand(m, @v)` reaches `m.send(@v)` in `hand`, which hands it over, line 26

## function hand_map

    function hand_map<A, B>(m: (mailbox {A: B}), @v: {A: B}) -> bool

Hands over a map whose keys or values hold a handle.

- `m`: the mailbox
- `v`: the map, emptied

    hand_map(m, @v)

What each call must give its type parameters (spec § 11):

- `A` and `B` reach no `ptr` and no `cstr`: `(mailbox {A: B})` makes it cross between threads, line 32
- `A` or `B` holds a C handle or a thread: `m.send(@v)` hands it over, line 33

## function start

    function start<S>(body: (function(S) -> i64), setup: S) -> i64

Runs `body` on `setup` on a thread of its own and waits for its answer.

- `body`: what the thread runs
- `setup`: what it runs on, copied to the thread

    start(body, setup)

What each call must give its type parameters (spec § 11):

- `S` reaches no `ptr` and no `cstr`: `spawn(body, setup)` makes it cross between threads, line 40
- `S` holds no C handle and no thread: `spawn(body, setup)` hands it to a new thread, line 40

## function fresh

    function fresh<T>(x: T) -> (mailbox T)

A new mailbox holding `x`.

- `x`: the first value it holds

    fresh(x)

What each call must give its type parameters (spec § 11):

- `T` reaches no `ptr` and no `cstr`: `(mailbox T)` makes it cross between threads, line 46
- `T` is not `()`: `(mailbox T)` is a mailbox of it, line 46
- `T` holds no C handle and no thread: `m.send(x)` copies it, line 48

## function either

    function either<T>(a: T, b: T, first: bool) -> T

`a` or `b`, asking nothing of their type.

- `a`: the first
- `b`: the second
- `first`: whether `a` is the answer

    either(a: a, b: b, first)
